.TH DE25-FPGA 8 "November 2026" "de25-nano-fpga-tools" "DE25-Nano"
.SH NAME
de25-fpga \- choose the core image loaded into the DE25-Nano's FPGA at boot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B de25-fpga list
.br
.B de25-fpga select
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.I name
.br
.B de25-fpga disable
.br
.B de25-fpga status
.SH DESCRIPTION
On a DE25-Nano set up for Altera's "HPS boot first" mode the chip is configured in two
phases. Phase 1 happens at power-on, from the HPS image in the board's flash: it sets up
the SoC and starts U-Boot. Phase 2 happens later, when U-Boot loads a
.I core image
into the FPGA from the SD card. The core image is the FPGA's logic and pins.
.PP
Several core images can be installed side by side.
Each comes in a package named
.BI de25-nano-core- name
and is kept in
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/core/ name\fR.
Exactly one of them, or none, is
.I selected
to be loaded at boot.
.B de25-fpga
makes that choice.
It never writes the flash; that is
.BR de25-flash (8).
.PP
A core image loads only on the HPS image it was built for.
Each core names the boot package
.RB ( de25-nano-boot-... )
whose HPS image it needs.
If the chip refuses a core image, Linux still starts, without the core, but the board
then cannot restart until its power has been unplugged and plugged in again.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List the installed cores, one a line: the name, whether it is selected and whether it is
running, and the boot package it needs.
.TP
.BI select " name"
Load the core
.I name
from the next boot on.
The command first checks that the core fits the flash.
It is taken to fit if the core now running needs the same boot package, if the flash
holds the HPS image of that boot package as installed, or if a flash write of that HPS
image is waiting for the next boot.
Otherwise the command changes nothing and says how to write the flash.
.TP
.B disable
Load no core from the next boot on.
.TP
.B status
Show the selected core and the running core.
If a core was selected but is not running, say why, as far as can be told.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-force
With
.BR select :
select the core without the check that it fits.
.SH UPGRADES
When the package of the selected core is upgraded, the new core image is put in place
by itself and is loaded at the next boot, as a new kernel is.
If the new version needs a different boot package, the card keeps the version it has,
and the upgrade says what to do.
Removing the package of the selected core has the effect of
.BR disable .
.SH WITHOUT A CORE
Without a core the board has its serial console and Ethernet, with the Ethernet PTP
clock.
The signals on the GPIO headers, the HDMI output and the control of the fan come with
the core.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/core/ name /fpga.rbf
The core image.
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/core/ name /fpga.dtbo
Its device tree overlay: what Linux must be told when the core is loaded.
.TP
.BI /usr/lib/de25-nano/core/ name /boot
The name of the boot package whose HPS image the core loads on.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/fpga.rbf
The selected core image, which U-Boot loads. Not there when no core is selected.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/fpga-core.txt
The name of the selected core.
.TP
.I /boot/dtbo/fpga.dtbo
The selected core's overlay, which the boot menu names. U-Boot applies it only when the
core image has loaded.
.TP
.I /proc/device-tree/chosen/de25-nano,core
The name of the running core, from its overlay.
.SH EXAMPLES
A board whose flash has been written with
.BR "de25-flash write" :
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo de25-fpga select base
sudo reboot
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR de25-flash (8),
.BR u-boot-update (8)
